# Inkling-2-Mini — a virtualized kernel declaration

This artifact declares how the Inkling-2-Mini transformer block
behaves, and carries the implementations of that behavior that exist.
It is the first instance of the unified-backend design: the
declaration and at least one implementation travel together, encoded
in multiversal phext, and a runner inflates the platform kernel it is
missing from the declaration plus whichever implementations it can
execute.

The model: Inkling-2-Mini, Thinking Machines, Apache 2.0 open
weights, released 2026-11-20. Architecture name in the artifact and
the runner: Inkling2LoopForConditionalGeneration (model_type
inkling2_loop). 26.4B parameters total, 3.4B active per token, 5.4B
effective weight activations once the loop's reuse is counted
(publisher's figures, model card).

Coordinate map of this artifact:
  1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.1  this scroll — home and map
  1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.2  declaration: structure and shapes
  1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.3  declaration: the loop
  1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.4  declaration: attention
  1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.5  declaration: mixture of experts
  1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.6  declaration: precision and dequantization
  1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.7  declaration: residual mixing
  1.1.1/1.2.1/1.1.1  implementation: the published MLX runner
  1.1.1/1.2.1/1.1.2  implementation: Linux CPU, this fork's patch
  1.1.1/1.2.1/1.1.3  run record: first inflation

Provenance: every number in the declaration scrolls was read from the
released weights artifact (config.json, hf_quant_config.json,
model.safetensors.index.json; SHA-256 verified against the
publisher's tree, 2026-11-21) or from this fork's runner source at
upstream HEAD 2bd4d6aabdf4caa39e7095e201dcda4cc1a3a406. Nothing here
is copied from marketing copy where the artifact itself speaks.
# Declaration — structure and shapes

Text tower (all dimensions from config.json):
  hidden_size            2304
  physical layers        24
  vocabulary             201024 (unpadded 200058)
  context length         1048576 (model_max_length)
  embedding              bf16, with its own norm
  unembedding            bf16 weights, float32 forward output
  end of sequence        token 200006

A forward pass is NOT 24 layer applications. Twelve of the
twenty-four layers sit inside a loop core and are applied three
times per token; the pass is 48 layer visits. The loop is declared
in scroll 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.3; it is graph structure, not a runtime
option: the same physical weights are re-applied, and each visit
owns separate cache state.

The artifact is multimodal: a vision tower is declared in the same
config (temporal_patch 2, native_patch 20, adapter_merge 2). This
declaration covers the text tower and the looped block, which is the
kernel the unified backend must inflate; the vision tower's behavior
is carried by the reference implementation's source and is not
re-declared here yet.

A multi-token-prediction draft head ships alongside (mtp.safetensors,
all bf16: a fused projection plus DFlare draft layers). It is an
optional speculation accelerator over this same block, not part of
the block's definition.
# Declaration — the loop

loop_config (from config.json):
  core_start    6
  core_end      18
  num_passes    3
  gate_rank     64
  is_varloop    false

Execution plan (the runner's executionPlan states it exactly):
  1. Apply layers [0, core_end) once: layers 0..17.
  2. For passes 2..num_passes, re-apply layers [core_start, core_end):
     layers 6..17, twice more.
  3. Apply layers [core_end, num_layers): layers 18..23.
Total layer visits per token: 18 + 12 + 12 + 6 = 48.

State rules:
  - A layer visit owns its own attention and convolution cache
    slots. Several visits can use the same physical layer's weights;
    they never share cache. Key/value state is therefore per-visit:
    the loop multiplies cache by the pass count for core layers, it
    does not amortize it.
  - Looping is only defined when the core boundaries align with
    residual blocks (n_layers_per_attn_res_block = 6; 6 and 18 are
    both block boundaries) and when residual values are normalized
    (attn_res_normalize_values = true). A config that violates
    either condition is not a looped model under this declaration.
  - Variable loop counts (is_varloop) are not supported by the
    reference runner and are outside this declaration.
  - At the first visit of a new pass (visit.layer == loop start),
    the residual stream passes through the loop's residual-mixing
    gate (gate_rank 64) before the layer applies. Declared in
    scroll 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.7.
# Declaration — attention

Per layer (from config.json):
  attention heads        24
  key/value heads        8 (grouped-query attention)
  head_dim               128
  q_bias, o_bias         false
  rms_norm_eps           1e-6
  use_value_norm_no_gamma  true

Hybrid pattern: twenty layers are LOCAL, four are GLOBAL.
  local_layer_ids: every layer except 5, 11, 17, 23
  global layers:   5, 11, 17, 23 — one per 6-layer residual block
Local layers attend inside a sliding window of 1024 tokens
(sliding_window_size); global layers attend without the window.
Local and global layers carry separate head counts in the config's
swa fields (also 24 query / 8 key-value, head_dim 128).

Relative position and scaling behavior:
  d_rel                  16 (learned relative-position features)
  rel_extent             1024
  sconv_kernel_size      4 (short convolution in the block)
  qk_l2_clip_threshold   22.627
  rel_l2_clip            8.0
  log_scaling_n_floor    8192
  alpha                  0.1
  final_logit_softcapping  30.0 (declared with the block; applied
                             at the unembedding)
# Declaration — mixture of experts

Every layer's feed-forward is a mixture of experts (from config.json):
  n_routed_experts       128
  num_experts_per_tok    8 (top-8 routing per token)
  n_shared_experts       0
  expert intermediate    1152 (w13 fused gate+up, w2 down)
  use_gate_bias          true
  route_scale            8.0
  mlp_gate_softcap       4.0
  mlp_up_softcap         25.0

Routing rule: the gate scores all 128 experts, applies its learned
bias, scales by route_scale, and selects the top 8; only those
experts' weights are read for the token. Active parameters per token
are 3.4B of 26.4B total; counting the loop's re-application of core
layers, effective weight activations are 5.4B per token.

Implementation note carried by the declaration: on GPU kernels the
runner routes tokens as sorted runs per expert (one launch per run);
on a backend without those kernels the same selection is computed
with ordinary graph operations. The selection rule is identical;
only the schedule differs. Expert weights are stored fused and
quantized — see the precision scroll, 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.6.
# Declaration — precision and dequantization

The checkpoint is trained and shipped quantized. No higher-precision
master weights exist: the quantized form IS the released precision,
and any conversion is a one-way widening of the same numbers, never
an upgrade. (Publisher's statement, confirmed by the artifact: the
safetensors hold only the forms below.)

Expert weights (from hf_quant_config.json):
  Layers 0-19 — NVFP4
    weight_transform     nvfp4_nearest
    weight_block_shape   [1, 16] — one scale per 16 values
    values               E2M1 (4-bit float)
    scales               float8_e4m3fn, format amax_denominator
    activation quant     per-token amax, block 16, format nvfp4_e2m1,
                         rounding nearest, scale selection
                         fouroversix_mae
  Layers 20-23 — MXFP8
    weight_transform     mxfp8_nearest
    weight_block_shape   [32, 32]
    values               E4M3 (8-bit float)
    scales               ue8m0 (power-of-two scales)
    activation quant     block 32, format mxfp8_e4m3, rounding nearest

Everything else is unquantized: attention, embeddings, norms and the
residual-mixing parameters in bf16 (the attention-residual group
computes and outputs in float32); the unembedding in bf16; the MTP
draft head entirely in bf16.

Dequantization rule (the only rule an implementation may use):
  NVFP4:  w = E2M1(code) * E4M3(scale)        per 16-value group
  MXFP8:  w = E4M3(code) * 2^(ue8m0 scale)    per 32x32 block
The runner's shared quant facts (mlx/quant in this fork) fix the
group sizes: nvfp4 groupSize 16, 4 bits; mxfp8 groupSize 32, 8 bits.
An implementation may dequantize inside its matmul kernel or ahead
of time into a wider format; both are the same declaration. What it
may not do is silently substitute a different quantization of the
weights — a requantized checkpoint is a different artifact, not an
implementation of this one.
# Declaration — residual mixing

The block's residual stream is managed in blocks of six layers
(n_layers_per_attn_res_block = 6; four blocks across 24 layers).
Each block carries learned attention-residual parameters
(attn_queries per block, in the float32 parameter group) that mix
earlier block outputs into the stream; residual values are
normalized before mixing (attn_res_normalize_values = true).

The loop interacts with the mixing at pass boundaries: when a new
pass begins (a visit whose layer equals the loop core start), the
runner applies dedicated loop-start handling that re-mixes the
residual stream through a low-rank gate (gate_rank = 64) before the
core layers re-apply. This is part of the block's behavior, not an
optimization: skipping it changes the function.

Schedules differ by backend and are recorded with each
implementation: the Metal implementation fuses this residual mixing
(upstream HEAD 2bd4d6aa is exactly that fusion); the CPU graph
implementation computes the same mixing as ordinary graph
operations. Behavior is the declaration; fusion is a schedule.
# Implementation — the published MLX runner (reference)

Where it lives: this fork, mlxrunner/model/inkling2 (model,
attention, experts, weights, config, vision, draft, residual, conv,
projection), with the importer at create/inkling2.go and shared quant
facts at mlx/quant. The converter classifies each expert bank by its
stored dtypes (NVFP4: weights uint8, scales E4M3; MXFP8: weights
E4M3, scales uint8) and rejects anything else.

Platforms as published: macOS on Metal (backends metal_v3,
metal_v4) and Linux/Windows on CUDA 13 (backend cuda_v13). The
Ollama library package for inkling-2-mini ships manifests for macOS
(32 GB and up) and DGX Spark only — no linux/amd64 manifest exists
upstream, which is the gap the litmus exists to close.

Kernel behavior: the mlx package carries per-backend GPU kernels
(nvfp4 matmul, matvec and activation kernels; sorted-run MoE
routing). A GPU kernel that cannot be created or launched on the
running backend disables itself permanently, and the model falls
back to computing the same outputs with graph operations. The
fallback is part of the published implementation's behavior — it is
what makes the CPU implementation below an inflation of this
declaration rather than a port.
# Implementation — Linux CPU (this fork's patch)

The missing platform kernel for the ranch is a CPU kernel: the
fleet has no discrete GPUs. The patch adds it where the fork's build
system dispatches MLX backends:

  cmake/local.cmake — a "cpu" branch in the OLLAMA_MLX_BACKENDS
    dispatch, calling ollama_add_mlx_build(cpu, PRESET mlx_cpu,
    RUNNER_DIR mlx_cpu) and appending target ollama-mlx-cpu.
  cmake/mlx/CMakePresets.json — an mlx_cpu preset: inherits the
    default preset, sets MLX_BUILD_CUDA=OFF and MLX_BUILD_METAL=OFF
    and OLLAMA_RUNNER_DIR=mlx_cpu. With CUDA and Metal off, MLX
    builds its CPU backend; libmlxc.so is produced for Linux.
  kernels/inkling2/ — this declaration artifact.

One Go source change proved necessary, in mlx/io.go: the
safetensors loader chose its load stream by operating system —
CPU stream on macOS, GPU stream everywhere else. On a CPU-only
build the GPU stream does not exist, the native load fails, and
the loader's iterator swallows the error, so every weight blob
silently yields zero tensors and the model reports its first
tensor missing. The fix selects the CPU stream whenever no GPU
device is available (GPUIsAvailable), which is the same fact the
rest of the package already branches on. With that, the MLX
loader accepts the CPU variant it dlopens, and the inkling2
model's GPU kernels decline to launch on the CPU backend while
the graph fallbacks execute — including NVFP4 and MXFP8
dequantization, performed in graph operations from the weights'
shipped packed form.

NVFP4 route chosen: in-kernel (in-graph) dequantization. The weights
stay NVFP4/MXFP8-packed in memory and in the model store; the
dequantization rule of scroll 1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.6 is applied as the
graph computes. Stated cost: dequantization arithmetic on every
matmul, paid in tokens per second, in exchange for zero precision
change and the smallest memory footprint. The build-time conversion
alternative (materialize bf16 experts ahead of time, roughly four
times the expert bytes) and requantization (rejected outright: it
would change the trained precision, and the release is the
precision) are costed in the litmus run record.
# Run record — first inflation

2026-11-21, elven-path (ranch node, Linux x86_64, 16 cores, 30 GB
RAM, no discrete GPU — AMD Phoenix iGPU only, unused). Backend:
MLX CPU (libmlxc.so built by this fork's patch, MLX version
0.32.4-82-gfc344c9, device=cpu per the runner's own log).

Before the patch's payload existed, the same binary answered the
create with: "MLX runtime is not available: failed to load MLX
dynamic library". After: the create imported inkling-2-mini with
1204 layers (19 GB in the model store), the runner loaded 1244
tensors from the manifest, and the model served through the normal
API on the node's ollama port.

Prompt 1 (raw completion, /api/generate): "What is the capital of
France? Answer in one sentence."
Output began: ", Paris is the capital of France." and continued
in a self-generated question-and-answer pattern, answering its own
follow-ups correctly (Washington, D.C.; Ottawa; Berlin; ...),
consuming the full 120-token allowance without an end-of-sequence
— ordinary raw-completion behavior without a chat template.
Coherent and factually correct throughout the sampled output.
Decode: 120 tokens in 672.0 s = 0.18 tokens per second. Prompt
processing: 12 tokens in about 25 s. Runner memory: 20.3 GB RSS
(the 19.5 GB packed weights plus caches and activations) against
the node's 30 GB.

Kernel-optimization look (the standing rule: model access always
pairs with one):
  - Dequantization strategy. Adopted: in-graph dequantization from
    the shipped packed form (zero precision change). Measured cost:
    it is the decode bottleneck — the graph fallback re-dequantizes
    the selected experts on every token (48 layer visits x top-8
    experts x about 8M parameters is roughly 3.1B parameters
    dequantized per token), which with the unfused graph schedule
    yields the 0.18 tok/s above. Rejected: build-time bf16
    materialization — the experts are 24.46B parameters, 48.9 GB
    at bf16, more RAM than any ranch node has (fleet maximum
    44 GB; this node 30 GB). Rejected: requantization — it would
    change the trained precision; the release is the precision.
  - Loop reuse. The graph already reuses the same physical weights
    across the 3 passes (that is the architecture), but per-visit
    cache ownership means key/value state is NOT shared across
    passes: 48 visits x 8 KV heads x 128 head_dim x 2 (K and V) x
    2 bytes is about 192 KB of cache per token. The residual-mix
    fusion the Metal backend has (upstream HEAD) has no CPU
    counterpart; the CPU path computes the same mixing as ordinary
    graph operations.
  - MoE routing. Top-8 of 128 experts per token per visit; the GPU
    sorted-run routing kernel self-disables on CPU and the graph
    fallback computes the same selection. Expert weight traffic per
    token is about 1.7 GB packed; the cost is dominated not by the
    traffic but by the per-token dequantization above.
  - Next optimization, sized but not built: a dequantized-expert
    cache. The node has about 8 GB of headroom over the packed
    weights; one expert in bf16 is about 16 MB, so the headroom
    holds roughly 500 of the 6144 expert slots, and routing locality
    across a sequence is high. Expected to remove most of the
    repeated dequantization; unmeasured until built.
  - Observed wart: the CPU variant's install directory also carries
    about 600 MB of bundled CUDA redistributable libraries (an
    upstream MLX install rule); the CPU backend neither loads nor
    needs them.

This scroll is appended to at each inflation: node, backend,
prompts, outputs, tokens per second, memory footprint, and the
kernel-optimization measurements.
